#  Frame-trace diagnostic (ATLANTIC_FRAME_TRACE=1, default OFF): CLOCK_MONOTONIC
#  marker at each WebProcess composite, paired with the qt5-plugin ui recv/paint/
#  ack markers to localize the franceinfo freeze-then-jump (production vs handoff
#  vs present). Touches ThreadedCompositor.cpp; apply after the other patches
#  touching it.
#
# ---
Frame-trace instrumentation to localize the scroll freeze (ATLANTIC_FRAME_TRACE).

Diagnostic-only (default OFF). Device frame-tracing localized the franceinfo
"content freezes then jumps" to a PRODUCTION stall: after the WebProcess acks a
frame, the next composite is delayed 1-3s (worst ~9.9s) during scroll - the
pipeline runs ~5.8fps in lockstep with multi-second gaps, all in the WebProcess
between ack and the next paintToCurrentGLContext. These extra markers pin the
cause of that ack->composite gap.

When ATLANTIC_FRAME_TRACE=1, emit CLOCK_MONOTONIC-stamped "[ftrace] web ..."
lines (same clock as the qt5-plugin "ui recv|paint|ack" markers):
  - ThreadedCompositor.cpp: "composite" (paintToCurrentGLContext) and
    "reqcomp r=<CompositionReason>" (requestComposition - what drives composites).
  - LayerTreeHost.cpp: "rupd w=<isWaitingForRenderer>" (main-thread rendering
    update runs) and "sched w=<isWaitingForRenderer>" (a rendering update is
    scheduled). w= exposes the composition<-tiles<-flush handshake flag.

So the ack->composite gap resolves to: no reqcomp/sched during the gap = the
scroll/update scheduler is starved; sched with w=1 = compositor waiting on the
renderer (tiles); rupd late/slow = main-thread update is the gate. Zero cost
when unset; not shipped on.
